          </div>

          <p>
            Those are typical nameplate ranges, not measurements of your house; check the labels on your own appliances or use our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a>. But the shape of the result holds for most homes: a running load in the mid-5,000s with a worst-case starting moment in the 7,000s. That is why the 8,000 watt class matters. A 5,000 or 6,000 watt unit runs the same appliances in rotation; an 8,000 watt unit runs them together and absorbs the pump start without the lights dipping or the breaker tripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other half of the job is how the power gets into the house. At this size you are not running extension cords through a window. You are plugging one cord from the generator's L14-30R twist-lock outlet into a power inlet box on the outside wall, which feeds a transfer switch or interlock at the panel, so the hardwired furnace and the 240V well pump can run at all. That connection has its own limit, and it happens to line up almost exactly with this size class, which is the next section.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size to the starting moment, not the running total.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most homes on this list run at 5,000 to 5,500 watts but peak above 7,000 when the well pump starts. An 8,000 watt generator covers that peak; a 6,000 watt one forces you to stagger loads by hand.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

