        <div id="thirty-amp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 30-Amp Ceiling: Why 8,000 Watts Is the Natural Limit
          </h2>

          <p>
            The L14-30R outlet on every generator here is a four-prong, 120/240V, 30-amp twist-lock. The math on it is fixed: 30 amps times 240 volts is 7,200 watts. That is the most power a 30-amp cord, inlet and transfer switch can move into your panel, no matter what the generator is rated for. A 12,000 watt generator plugged into a 30-amp inlet is a 7,200 watt generator with extra weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why this class is the sweet spot for a 30-amp setup. The Westinghouse WGen7500DFc is rated for 7,500 running watts and the Generac GP8000E for 8,000, so both can deliver the full 7,200 watts the connection allows while running comfortably below their own limits. The WEN DF8000X, at 6,500 rated watts on gasoline, fits inside the 30-amp limit with a margin for the inlet. If you already own a 30-amp inlet and transfer switch, an 8,000 watt generator uses all of it, and nothing bigger gains you usable power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Short surges are a different matter. Starting watts last a second or two, and the 30-amp breakers in the generator and the transfer switch are thermal-magnetic devices that tolerate brief inrush. So a 10,000 starting-watt rating does help the well pump start even through a 30-amp cord. What you cannot do is run more than about 7,200 watts continuously through it, and in practice you should plan for 80 percent of that, around 5,800 watts, for loads that run for hours. That number covers the running total from the table above for most houses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once your plan needs more than that, the connection has to change, not just the generator. A 50-amp inlet and a 14-50R outlet raise the ceiling to 12,000 watts, which is where our guides to the <a href="/best-10000-watt-generator/">best 10,000 watt generators</a> and the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generators with a 50 amp outlet</a> take over. If your list includes central air or an electric water heater, start there. If it stops at the window unit and the well pump, a 30-amp inlet and an 8,000 watt generator is the cheaper, lighter and simpler answer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
