        <div id="dual-fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gas, Propane or Both at 8,000 Watts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four of the five picks here are dual fuel, and at this size that is the right default. The reason is not cost per kilowatt-hour; propane usually costs more per hour of runtime. It is storage. Gasoline goes stale in months unless treated and is hard to buy when the stations have no power. Propane in a 20 or 40 pound cylinder keeps for years, and most people with a gas grill already own one or two tanks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade is output. Propane carries less energy per unit than gasoline, so every dual fuel generator is rated lower on it. Across this list the drop runs from about 5 to 10 percent: the DuroMax XP9500iH goes from 7,600 to 7,200 running watts, the Westinghouse WGen7500DFc from 7,500 to 6,750, the WEN DF8000X from 6,500 to 5,850, and the Champion 201175 from 7,000 to 6,300. For a house that peaks at 7,000 watts on pump starts, that drop can matter, so read the propane column before you buy if propane is your main fuel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold weather favours propane for starting, but only up to a point. A 20 pound cylinder feeding a generator at half load is pulling vapour quickly, and in deep cold the tank can frost and lose pressure. Owners commonly report that a 40 pound cylinder, or two 20s swapped through the night, avoids that. If you live somewhere that sees single-digit winter temperatures and you need the furnace blower to run, plan around bigger cylinders, not the one under your grill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas-only generators like the Generac GP8000E are still worth considering if you already rotate gasoline for a tractor or mower and store it with stabiliser. They are simpler, often deliver their full rating, and avoid the regulator and hose. But if you do not have a reliable fuel routine, buy dual fuel and keep three or four propane cylinders filled. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual fuel generator guide</a> covers the fuel trade in more depth, and <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">how much propane a generator uses</a> has the cylinder math.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-burn" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fuel Burn at 50% Load: What a Day Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Half load is the number to plan with, because a house running a fridge, freezer, furnace and lights sits around 3,500 to 4,500 watts most of the time, which is roughly half of these generators' running ratings. Manufacturers publish runtime at 50 percent load for exactly this reason. Divide tank size by that runtime and you get a burn rate you can plan a weekend around.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Model</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tank</th>
