          </div>

          <p>
            The striking result is how close they are. Whether the engine is the WEN's 340cc single or a 420cc to 459cc unit, at half load these machines burn about two-thirds of a gallon an hour, which is 15 to 16 gallons a day if you run them around the clock. That is three or four 5-gallon cans a day. Most people do not run continuously; running six hours on and two off, keeping the freezer and fridge cold, cuts that to about 10 gallons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane is heavier going. DuroMax's sheet for the XP9500iH lists 10 hours at half load on a 40 pound cylinder, about 4 pounds an hour. A standard 20 pound grill tank therefore lasts around five hours at half load, and a day of continuous running needs roughly 100 pounds of propane, five grill tanks. That is the honest cost of propane's long shelf life, and why owners on wells often install a 100 pound cylinder and a regulator rather than juggling 20s.
          </p>

          <p>
            Champion and Westinghouse publish runtime at 25 percent load rather than 50: up to 14 hours on the Champion 201175's 5.33 gallon tank and up to 11 hours on the Westinghouse's 6.6 gallon tank. Quarter-load figures flatter everyone, so do not compare them directly with the half-load numbers above. As a rule of thumb, open-frame engines at half load burn a bit under twice what they burn at a quarter. The inverter models, which throttle the engine down at light load, gain more at a quarter and less at half.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">PLAN THE FUEL</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Budget about 10 gallons or 60 pounds of propane per day.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That assumes half load with the generator off for a few hours overnight. Store at least three days of it before storm season, and rotate gasoline with stabiliser every six months.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Inverter or Open Frame at This Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two of the picks, the Champion 201175 and the DuroMax XP9500iH, are inverter generators in enclosed housings. The other three are conventional open-frame machines. At 2,000 watts the inverter is the obvious choice; at 8,000 watts it is a real decision, because the price gap is large and the advantages shrink when the generator is sitting 20 feet from the house feeding a panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            The inverter case is noise and power quality. Champion rates the 201175 at 64 dBA from 23 feet and DuroMax rates the XP9500iH at 61 dBA from 23 feet at quarter load. Open-frame units in this class are typically rated in the low to mid 70s, and each 10 dB is roughly a doubling of perceived loudness, so an inverter is about half as loud to your neighbours. Inverters also produce clean, low-distortion output, which matters for furnaces with electronic control boards and for variable-speed equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The open-frame case is cost, simplicity and repairability. A conventional 8,000 watt generator usually costs considerably less than a comparable inverter, uses a common engine and carburetor with parts sold everywhere, and does nothing clever that can fail. Modern open-frame units with automatic voltage regulation run most furnace boards without trouble, though owners commonly report that some high-efficiency furnaces are fussy about generator power. If yours is, an inverter solves it.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical difference: weight. Neither class is portable in the carry-it sense. The WEN DF8000X is the lightest here at about 139 pounds with its wheel kit, and everything else is over 200 pounds. Inverters at this size are not lighter. They roll on wheels like the rest, so pick the storage spot and a level path to the running spot before you buy. For a full breakdown of the two technologies, see our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator</a> comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-starts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Starting a Well Pump and a Window AC on the Same Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The scenario that sinks undersized generators is two motors asking to start in the same second. It happens more than you would expect: the well pump kicks on when someone runs a tap, at the same time the thermostat calls the window AC. The generator sees the full running load plus both inrush currents at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Starting-watt ratings are the defence, and this is where the numbers on the box diverge. The Generac GP8000E is rated for 10,000 starting watts and Generac markets PowerRush to raise its motor-starting capacity. The DuroMax XP9500iH is rated for 9,500 peak, the Westinghouse for 9,500, the Champion 201175 for 8,500 and the WEN for 8,000. If your house has a 1.5 HP well pump or a larger AC, the higher starting ratings are worth paying attention to.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also cut the problem down without a bigger generator. A soft starter on the window AC, a few dollars to a hundred dollars depending on the unit, can cut its inrush substantially. A well pump with a pressure tank only cycles when the pressure drops, so it rarely coincides with anything else for long. And the transfer switch itself helps: with each circuit on its own breaker, you can leave the AC off until the pump and fridge are running, then switch it on.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 240V part matters too. Most deep-well pumps run on 240V, which is only available from a generator through its L14-30R or 14-50R outlet and a transfer switch that brings both legs into the panel. If your well pump is on 240V and your generator's 240V outlet is only reachable by an adapter cord, fix the hookup before the storm. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">what size generator runs a well pump</a> walks through horsepower to watts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Hooking It Up: Transfer Switch, Inlet and Cord
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every generator on this list has the L14-30R outlet you need; the house side is where the decisions are. There are three common setups. A manual transfer switch, a small subpanel next to the main with six to ten selected circuits, is the most common and the easiest for an electrician to install. A breaker interlock kit, a sliding plate that physically prevents the main and the generator breaker from being on at the same time, lets you run any circuit in the house. And an automatic transfer switch, rarer with portables, starts the swap without you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you pick, the run is the same: generator L14-30R outlet, a 10-gauge four-wire L14-30 cord, a weatherproof power inlet box on the outside wall, and a 30-amp double-pole breaker or the transfer switch inside. Never back-feed through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord; it energises the utility lines and can kill a line worker. Installed by an electrician, a manual transfer switch or an interlock kit usually costs a few hundred to around a thousand dollars; our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> covers the trade-offs, and our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">manual transfer switch picks</a> cover the hardware.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the cord short and heavy. At 30 amps a 25 foot 10-gauge cord loses very little; a 50 foot run is fine at 10-gauge but is the practical limit before you should step up to 8-gauge. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">extension cord sizing guide</a> has the voltage-drop table. The cord length matters because of where the generator has to sit.
          </p>

          <p>
            That spot is at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, on a level surface, and somewhere you can reach to refuel in the dark. All five generators here have a CO shutdown sensor, which is a backup, not a reason to run one in a garage or under a deck. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers rain and snow. Remote start, on the Westinghouse and the DuroMax, earns its money here: you can start the generator from the house without walking out in the weather.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bigger" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. 8,000 vs 10,000 vs 12,000 Watts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The next sizes up are not just bigger versions of this one. They exist to feed a different connection. A 10,000 or 12,000 watt generator delivers its extra power through a 14-50R 50-amp outlet, and you only get that power if the inlet, cord and transfer switch are all rated for 50 amps. Plug a 12,000 watt unit into a 30-amp inlet and it behaves like an 8,000 watt unit that weighs 50 pounds more and burns more fuel at the same load.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the decision is really about loads. If your outage list ends at the window AC and the well pump, 8,000 watts on a 30-amp hookup is the right size, and stepping up buys you headroom you cannot use. If you want to add an electric water heater, a 3-ton central air conditioner, an electric dryer or a well pump over 1.5 HP, the running total climbs past what a 30-amp connection carries, and you should be shopping at 10,000 to 12,000 watts with a 50-amp inlet. Our <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt generator guide</a> starts there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel is the hidden cost of oversizing. A larger engine at a light load is less efficient than a smaller engine at half load, so a 12,000 watt open-frame unit feeding a 4,000 watt house load burns noticeably more per hour than an 8,000 watt one doing the same job. Over a five-day outage that difference is real money and several extra fuel runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going down a size has its own trade. A 5,000 to 6,000 watt generator will run the same appliances, but in rotation: fridge and freezer on, then off while the well pump fills the pressure tank, then the window AC at night. That works, and many households live with it. Our <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">5,000 watt generator guide</a> covers that approach. The 8,000 watt class is for people who would rather not manage the panel all night.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip This Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip it if your outages are short and rare. If the power goes out for four hours twice a year, a 2,000 to 4,000 watt inverter to keep the fridge cold and the router on is cheaper, quieter and much easier to store. Our guide to the <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">best small generators for home</a> covers that tier, and a <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">portable power station</a> handles even more of it without fuel at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you cannot install a transfer switch or interlock. Without the house connection, an 8,000 watt generator can only run things through extension cords from its 120V outlets, which leaves the hardwired furnace and the 240V well pump out of reach, the two loads that justify this size. If you rent or cannot get an electrician, buy something smaller that you will actually be able to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you need to move it often. Every generator here except the WEN weighs over 200 pounds, which means a two-person lift into a truck bed. These are home backup machines that live on wheels in a garage or shed. For RV and campsite use, a smaller inverter is the right tool; our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">RV generator guide</a> covers that.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip it if you want the house to look after itself. If outages are frequent and long where you live, or nobody at home can safely refuel a generator in a storm, a standby generator on natural gas or a large propane tank is the better answer. It costs much more but starts itself. Our <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable vs standby comparison</a> lays out the costs, and our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> roundup covers every other size if you are still deciding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more than the 30-amp cord can carry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An L14-30 cord carries 7,200 watts, full stop. A 12,000 watt generator on a 30-amp inlet does not give you more usable power, only more weight and fuel burn. If your plan needs more, change the inlet and cord to 50 amps first, then buy a bigger generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the surge number as the running number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several 8,000 watt generators are 8,000 watts only for a second or two. The WEN DF8000X runs at 6,500 on gas. Compare running watts on the fuel you plan to use, and size your house load against that, not the big number on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the propane derate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every dual fuel model loses output on propane, from about 5 to 10 percent across this list. If you plan to run mostly on propane, read the propane running-watt figure, and keep 40 pound cylinders for cold nights, not just a single grill tank.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Back-feeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord into a dryer outlet energises the utility lines and can kill a line worker or start a fire. Use a power inlet box and a transfer switch or an interlock installed by an electrician. It costs a few hundred dollars and makes the setup legal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing too little fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At half load these generators burn about two-thirds of a gallon an hour, around 10 to 15 gallons a day. One 5 gallon can is half a day. Store three days of fuel before storm season, stabilise the gasoline and rotate it every six months.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will an 8000 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, a refrigerator, chest freezer, gas furnace blower, a 1 HP well pump, one window air conditioner, lights and electronics at the same time. That load typically runs around 5,000 watts and peaks above 7,000 when the pump starts. It will not run central air, an electric water heater or an electric dryer alongside everything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an 8000 watt generator run a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a whole house with central air and electric appliances. It runs the essentials and comforts of most homes through a transfer switch, but through a 30-amp L14-30 connection it is capped at 7,200 watts. Whole-house coverage with central air usually needs a 50-amp portable at 12,000 watts or more, or a standby generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is 8000 watts enough for a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for most residential well pumps up to about 1.5 HP. A 1 HP 240V pump typically needs 3,000 to 4,000 watts to start, and this class has 8,000 to 10,000 starting watts. The pump must be fed at 240V through the L14-30R outlet and a transfer switch, not from a 120V outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will an 8000 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 10 to 12 hours at half load on the 6.7 to 7.9 gallon tanks in this class, based on manufacturer ratings. That works out to around two-thirds of a gallon an hour. At quarter load the same tank lasts longer, around 14 to 21 hours, depending on the model and whether it is an inverter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much propane does an 8000 watt generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 4 pounds an hour at half load, based on DuroMax's rating of 10 hours on a 40 pound cylinder for the XP9500iH. That means a standard 20 pound grill tank lasts about five hours at half load, and a full day of continuous running needs around 100 pounds of propane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between an 8000 and an 8500 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the label, not the output. Many 8,500 watt generators, like the Champion 201175, are rated 8,500 starting and 7,000 running, while some 8,000 watt models are rated 8,000 running and 10,000 starting. Compare running watts on your fuel, not the model name, because the bigger number can be the smaller generator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size transfer switch do I need for an 8000 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30-amp transfer switch or interlock with a 30-amp inlet and a 10-gauge L14-30 cord. That matches the L14-30R outlet on every generator in this class and carries up to 7,200 watts. A 50-amp switch only makes sense if you plan to move up to a generator with a 14-50R outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

